course: biology
lesson: 285
title: What a child can do for nature
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: You do not need to be a grown-up scientist to help wild animals. A messy corner of a garden, a dish of water on a hot day or a small hole in a fence can save lives every night.
explain: Think of a hedgehog walking through gardens after dark: it may travel a kilometre or two looking for beetles and worms, and a solid fence is a wall it cannot pass. One hole the size of a CD opens a whole street for it.
explain: The best help is often to leave things alone: watch a frog instead of taking it home, and let a baby bird on the ground be fed by its parents.
explain: Small choices add up when millions of people make them: lights dimmed at night, food not wasted, native flowers planted, sightings sent to scientists and polite letters sent to the council.
fact: Long grass, wildflowers and a log pile give homes to insects, frogs and hedgehogs, so a wild corner holds more life than a tidy lawn.
fact: A small hole about 13 centimetres wide, roughly the size of a CD, at the bottom of a fence lets hedgehogs travel between gardens.
fact: A shallow dish of water with a stone in it helps birds, bees and hedgehogs in dry summers; the stone lets insects climb out, and the dish should be cleaned often, because dirty water spreads disease; never give hedgehogs milk, which upsets their stomachs.
fact: Sea turtles can mistake floating plastic bags for jellyfish and swallow them; litter picks on beaches should be done with gloves and with adults.
fact: Bird feeders help most in winter and must be kept clean; bread and salty food are poor food for birds and ducks, because bread fills them up without giving the goodness they need.
fact: Wild animals should be watched and photographed, not taken home: a pond frog can die in a tank, and moving frogs between ponds spreads disease.
fact: Many baby birds found on the ground are fledglings that have left the nest before they can fly well, and their parents are still feeding them nearby.
fact: A fledgling is usually best left alone or put in a bush close by; a rescue should be called only if it is hurt or in danger, for example from a cat.
fact: Bright lights at night confuse moths and migrating birds, and baby sea turtles crawl towards lights instead of towards the sea.
fact: Native flowers feed local insects best, and some caterpillars can eat only one kind of plant.
fact: Apps let children record the animals and plants they see; their photos let experts check what each animal really is and help scientists map where species live.
fact: Saving food, water and energy and reusing bottles and bags means less land cleared and less pollution.
fact: Councils can make wild verges, plant trees and turn lights down, and many changes start with a polite letter from a child.
exercise: Why does a wild corner of the garden help nature? | Long grass, flowers and logs give homes to insects, frogs and hedgehogs | It keeps all wild animals out of the garden, so the flowers are safe | Wild corners grow more vegetables to eat | Messy places are warmer for people
exercise: What does a hedgehog highway mean? | A small hole at the bottom of a fence so hedgehogs can pass between gardens | A busy road with a big sign that warns all the drivers to look out for hedgehogs | A tunnel dug under the house for hedgehogs to sleep | A path of food laid across the lawn
exercise: About how big should a hole for hedgehogs be? | About the size of a CD | About the size of a coin | About the size of a door | About the size of a football pitch
exercise: Why put a stone in a dish of water for wildlife? | So insects that fall in can climb out | So the water stays nice and cold all day | So birds have something to peck and eat | So the dish does not blow away in rain
exercise: Why can a plastic bag in the sea kill a sea turtle? | The turtle can mistake it for a jellyfish and eat it | The bag makes the water around the turtle far too hot | The turtle uses it as a shell | The bag scares away all the fish the turtle eats
exercise: Why is bread a poor main food for ducks? | It fills them up without giving the goodness they need | It is too heavy and makes them sink | Ducks are allergic to all wheat | Bread makes their feathers fall out in clumps by the summer
exercise: You find a frog in a pond and want to keep it. What is best? | Watch it, take a photo and leave it there | Take it home in a big jar full of tap water | Move it to a different pond | Keep it in your bedroom for a week or two
exercise: You find a feathered baby bird hopping on the ground. What is usually best? | Leave it or put it in a bush nearby, as its parents still feed it | Take it home and feed it bread | Put it back as high in a tree as you can climb, so that it is safe from cats | Give it water from a spoon
exercise: When should you call an animal rescue for a baby bird? | When it is hurt or in danger | Whenever you see it on the ground | Only if it is singing loudly at you | Never, rescues do not help birds
exercise: Why do baby sea turtles get lost near bright beach lights? | They crawl towards the light instead of towards the sea | Lights make them fall asleep on the sand before they reach the water | Lights make the sand too hot | They are scared of the dark sea
exercise: Why plant native flowers instead of only foreign ones? | Local insects feed best on them, and some caterpillars need one plant | Native flowers never need water | Foreign flowers are poisonous to people and pets, so they must never be planted | Native flowers grow only in winter
exercise: How can a nature app help scientists? | Photos of sightings show where animals and plants live | It makes the wild animals come straight into your garden | It feeds the animals for you | It tells animals where people live
exercise: How does wasting less food help wild animals? | Less land has to be cleared to grow food | Wasted food is eaten by every wild animal safely | Food waste makes the forests grow much faster | It does not help the wild animals in any way
exercise: A child wants a wild verge on her street. What can she do? | Write a polite letter to the council | Dig up the whole road herself with a spade | Pull out all the grass on the verge at night | Wait quietly until she is a grown-up adult
exercise: Why turn off garden lights at night? | Bright lights confuse moths and birds | Lights make the plants grow far too fast at night | Moths eat light bulbs when they are left on | The dark keeps hedgehogs away from the garden
exercise: Why should a wildlife water dish be cleaned often? | Dirty water can spread disease among animals | Clean water brings in more cats to the garden | Animals will only drink water that sparkles in the sun | It stops the stone from moving about in the dish
test: «dad says tidy lawn = good garden, mow everything» What would help nature more? | Leaving one corner long and wild with a log pile | Mowing twice as often | Spraying the whole lawn against all insects, good and bad | Covering the grass with stones
test: «there's a tiny bird on the path, no flying, just hopping, gonna take it home and save it» What is right? | It is probably a fledgling being fed by its parents, so leave it close by | Yes, it needs to live in a box at home | Throw it high into the air so it learns to fly, like its parents do with it | Feed it milk and bread at once
test: Count them: 3 hedgehogs pass through the fence hole. How many legs and how many noses? | 12 legs and 3 noses | 9 legs and 3 noses | 12 legs and 12 noses | 6 legs and 3 noses
test: What would happen if every garden on a street had a solid fence with no gaps? | Hedgehogs would be trapped in one garden and could not find enough food | Hedgehogs would have more food each | Hedgehogs would climb over every fence easily | Nothing, hedgehogs never leave one garden
test: «turtles are thick, who eats a bag lol» Why do they do it? | A floating bag looks like a jellyfish, their real food | Turtles like the taste of plastic | Turtles eat everything they see that is blue | Turtles want to build nests with it on the beach
test: In a cartoon a girl keeps a pond frog in a goldfish bowl on her desk and it sings every day. What is real? | A wild frog taken from its pond often dies, so it is better left there | Frogs live happily in a bowl for years, as long as you sing to them each day | Frogs prefer tap water to ponds | Frogs need no food in a bowl
test: «one kid turning lights off won't save anything, waste of time» What is right? | Small choices by many people add up to a big difference | Yes, only scientists in laboratories can help nature | Turning lights off harms birds | Lights do not affect animals at all
test: Why might a garden full of fancy foreign flowers have fewer butterflies than a wild meadow? | Many caterpillars can eat only certain native plants | Butterflies do not like colours | Foreign flowers grow far too tall for butterflies to reach the tops | Butterflies only live in towns
test: «found a hedgehog on the lawn at night, should i give it a saucer of milk» What is right? | No, milk upsets its stomach; a shallow dish of water is better | Yes, milk is its favourite drink, so a big saucer every night is best | Yes, warm milk with bread is best | No, hedgehogs never drink anything
test: «picked up 20 bits of plastic on the beach, 6 were bottle tops — no wait, 8. how many weren't bottle tops» | 12 | 14 | 20 | 8
test: Why does a photo matter when you report a rare beetle to a nature app? | Experts can check what it really is | It makes the beetle bigger | It tells the beetle you are there | Apps do not accept words
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the provision of an aperture at the base of a garden fence is recommended. | To permit the passage of hedgehogs between adjoining gardens in search of food | To permit the drainage of rainwater from the lawn | To facilitate the entry of domestic cats | To deter foxes from the property
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a juvenile bird discovered upon the ground. | It is frequently a fledgling still attended by its parents and is best left in situ | It is invariably abandoned and requires removal to a dwelling for hand-rearing | It must be returned forthwith to the highest available branch | It should be fed bread and milk without delay
exam: Ascertain the reason for which the reduction of artificial illumination at night is advised. | Bright lights disorient moths, migrating birds and hatchling turtles | Artificial light accelerates the growth of weeds | Darkness increases the population of rodents | Illumination causes flowers to close prematurely
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the value of planting native flowering species? | They furnish the most suitable food for local insects, some of which depend on a single plant | They require no water whatsoever | They exclude all insects from the garden | They are exclusively of ornamental value
